body:
Transferable in its current form is a weird beastie: it's a mixin, but we're trying to also use it as an argument type... in theory.  In practice, Gecko's IDL has:

```
typedef any Transferable;
```

and then failures just pop up during processing as needed (there's basically a switch over what sort of thing the `any` is, and if it's not one of the types we handle then we get an exception (a not very helpful one, actually, as it turns out)).  In Blink, we have:

```
// Expressing the Transferable typedef in IDL depends on http://crbug.com/240176.
// The postMessage() methods taking a Transferable array argument have custom
// binding code that is able to handle the Transferables that we currently
// recognize. To be able to declare a postMessage() signature that matches
// the implementation, we provide a Transferable typedef but with an
// incomplete type.
typedef MessagePort Transferable;
```

I'm not sure where V8Window::postMessageMethodCustom fits in in relation to the IDL spec's processing model, but it sure looks to me like it handles processing of the transferable bit before it does the `ToString` on the target origin, which matches neither the spec nor Gecko...

Anyway, I think there are two principled things we could do here: make the type `any` in the IDL and just have a processing model in prose, or define `Transferable` to be a union.  The latter does mean that you can't just add to the union via `implements Transferable` but that's _already_ broken for transferable things that are not IDL interfaces, such as array buffers, right?

